<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kara-Khanid Khanate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 840–<a href="/years/1212">1212</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Qarakhanids, Karakhanids, Qarakhanid Khanate, Kara-Khanids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kara-Khanid Khanate was a Turkic dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> from about the ninth century until 1212, the first Turkic dynasty to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the tenth century. In 999 it took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and ended the Samanid state, dividing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> with the Ghaznavids. Later split into eastern and western khanates, it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a> before falling to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmshah">Khwarazmshahs</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kara-Khanid Khanate grew from a confederation formed in the 9th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karluks">Karluks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaghma">Yagmas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chigils">Chigils</a>, Tuhsi and other peoples living in Zhetysu, the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>. The Karluks were nomads from the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai Mountains</a> who, by 766, had forced the Turgesh to submit and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suyab">Suyab</a> on the Chu their capital. At the end of the 8th century they converted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>. The date of its foundation and the name of its first khan are uncertain. By one reconstruction the first ruler was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilge-kul-qadir-khan">Bilge Kul Qadir Khan</a>. Its history is reconstructed from fragmentary and often contradictory written sources and from studies of coinage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the mid-10th century the Kara-Khanids converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and adopted Muslim names while keeping Turkic titles such as Khan and Ilek. Between 990 and 992 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-bughra-khan">Hasan Bughra Khan</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, though he died in 992 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> returned. By 999 Nasr, son of Ali, had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and Bukhara. The Samanid lands were divided between the Ghaznavids, who gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, and the Karakhanids, who received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, so that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> became the boundary.</p><p>The state was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasagun">Balasagun</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzgen">Uzgen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, and internal warfare led to a formal split into Eastern and Western khanates in the 1040s. In 1089 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> took Samarkand, and the Western Khanate was their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> for about half a century. In 1141 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a> defeated the Seljuk Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar">Ahmad Sanjar</a> and the Kara-Khanids at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-qatwan">Battle of Qatwan</a> near Samarkand. The Karakhanids stayed in power as Qara Khitai vassals, serving as tax collectors in Samarkand and Kashgar. In 1212 the Khwarazmshah Muhammad executed the Western ruler Uthman and extinguished the Western Khanate, and the Eastern line ended when rebels killed Prince Yusuf in Kashgar in 1211.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The court language at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> and other centres, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khaqani">Khaqani</a>, remained Turkic, though court culture was almost entirely Persian. Mahmud al-Kashgari wrote the first comprehensive dictionary of Turkic languages, the Dīwānu l-Luġat al-Turk, in Arabic in 1072-76, and Yusuf Balasaghuni wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutadgu-bilig">Kutadgu Bilig</a>, a work of advice for princes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-tamghach-khan">Ibrahim Tamghach Khan</a> founded the earliest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalyan">Kalyan</a> minaret in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, built by Muhammad Arslan Khan, survives. Rulers used titles such as Tamghaj Khan, meaning Khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and the Cambridge World History describes the state as the first of the Islamic Turco-Iranian states.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kara-Khanid Khanate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Sabuktigin's death Mahmud overthrew his brother Ismail in 998, and in 999, as the Samanid state collapsed between the Ghaznavids and the <b>Qarakhanids</b>, he made himself master of Khurasan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1137 he defeated the Western <b>Karakhanids</b> near Khujand and annexed Fergana and Tashkent, his army swollen by 10,000 Khitans formerly subject to the Karakhanids.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Malik Shah I (r. 1072–1092) the empire campaigned across Syria, Anatolia, the Caucasus and Central Asia and received the submission of the <b>Karakhanids</b> of Samarkand and Kashgar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Karluks, with the Chigils and Yagmas, later founded the <b>Kara-Khanid Khanate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the west, the <b>Karakhanids</b>, who ruled Kashgar and the lands around it from the 10th century, became the first Turkic dynasty to adopt Islam, and their language, close to Old Turkic, became a language of Islamic literature written in Arabic script.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-turkic">Old Turkic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
